<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramana Maharshi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Venkataraman Iyer, Bhagavan Sri Ramana Maharshi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramana Maharshi, born Venkataraman Iyer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, was an Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> sage whom his followers and most advocates of Advaita regarded as a liberated being. In 1896, at the age of 16, he had a death-experience in which he became aware of his true self, and six weeks later he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> for the holy mountain Arunachala at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruvannamalai">Tiruvannamalai</a>, where he stayed for the rest of his life. An ashram, Sri Ramanasramam, grew up around him from 1922. He taught self-enquiry, set out in Who am I?, as the main path, with devotion or surrender. Paul Brunton's A Search in Secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> of 1934 made him known in the West, and he died in 1950 after a cancerous growth on his arm.</p></div>
